lesson.body:
Print debugging works by editing your code to ask it questions, one print statement at a time, then rerunning the whole program to get the answer. A debugger is a tool, usually built into your IDE, that lets you ask those questions of a program that is already running, pausing it mid execution so you can look around without changing a single line of code.

This is a direct extension of the general debugging process: instead of the crude but reliable technique of printing values and rereading output, a debugger gives you a live, interactive view of the program's state at the exact line you care about. The core pieces of a debugger are breakpoints, which mark the line where execution should pause, stepping controls, which let you move forward one line or one function call at a time once paused, a variables pane, which shows every variable currently in scope and its value without you having to print each one individually, and a call stack view, which shows the chain of function calls that led to where you currently are. The next two chapters go into breakpoints and stepping in detail; this chapter is about what the tool as a whole gives you.

The advantage becomes obvious once a bug involves more moving parts than a single loop. Consider a recursive function meant to compute Fibonacci numbers:

def fib(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 1)

This has a bug: the second recursive call should be fib(n - 2), not fib(n - 1). Calling fib(4) does not crash; it just returns the wrong number, and because the function calls itself several times with different values of n, print statements would produce a tangle of output that is hard to read in order. A debugger handles this better: you set a breakpoint on the return line, run fib(4), and each time execution pauses there, you can look at the call stack pane to see the full chain of calls, fib(4) called fib(3), which called fib(2), and so on, with the current value of n shown for every frame at once. You quickly notice both recursive calls are using n - 1 instead of one of them using n - 2, something that would take much longer to spot by scattering print statements across recursive calls.

Debuggers exist for essentially every language and editor, and while the exact menus differ, the ideas of pausing at a breakpoint, stepping through code, and inspecting variables and the call stack are the same everywhere, which is why they are worth learning as concepts and not just as one tool's specific keyboard shortcuts.
